# round c5 (master 807eaa5 + seam2b, dev :5294, CDP 9844), timestamp-pdf.
# Re-settled on the merged tree with the committed rigs — `timestamp-round.mjs` (now
# also measuring the request from both sides) and `timestamp-read.py` (token, chain,
# imprint target, ink parity). The chain itself was fixed in an earlier round and is
# NOT re-derived here; what is re-measured, and what was found broken, is below.

CHAIN, signed file → timestamp (fixture /tmp/r/dig-a.pdf 23 275 B → /tmp/r/ts-on-diga.pdf):
  byte_range_unchanged            True   [0 5161 21545 1730], file length unchanged
  covers_to_eof True · bytes_outside_ranges 0 · gap_is_the_contents_placeholder 16384
  cms_verify_rc                   0      "CMS Verification successful" AFTER the stamp
  pdfsig                          "Signature is Valid." + "Total document signed"
  token_bytes                     5997   parses (`openssl ts -reply -text`)
  imprint_is_sha256_of_signature_value   True   (128-byte signature value, not the pages)
  ts_verify_over_signature_value_rc      0      "Verification: OK" against the
                                                 responder chain + DigiCert root
  ts_verify_over_covered_bytes_rc        1      control: the stamp is NOT over the document
  gen_time Nov 25 18:07:56 2026 GMT · policy 2.16.840.1.114412.7.1 · responder
  "DigiCert SHA256 RSA4096 Timestamp Responder 2026 1" · der_bytes 891 → 6917 inside the
  same placeholder · ink equal page for page (8 pages, 1 913 text chars, [61153, 39376,
  0, 142563, 249900, 0, 24192, 24192] before and after)
  → `validate-signature-pdf` on the stamped file: "All 1 signature(s) verify" (Step 3).

NETWORK HALF, measured by the rig on every run (page side + wire side):
  first stamp   : fetch POST https://rfc3161.ai.moda  70 B  head
                  `30 44 02 01 01 30 31 30 0d 06 09 60` = SEQUENCE { INTEGER 1 (version),
                  SEQUENCE { SEQUENCE { OID 2.16.840.1.101.3.4.2.1 (sha256), NULL },
                  OCTET STRING … } } — a `TimeStampReq`, and `startsWithPdf: false`.
                  Wire: OPTIONS (CORS preflight) + POST. Nothing else.
  named stamp   : same shape, 69 B (nonce length varies by a byte).
  unreachable   : same 69 B handed to fetch, wire shows only the OPTIONS attempt.
  So: the digest of the signature value is the only thing that leaves the tab, exactly
  as the card says. (The earlier network audit's ~70 B figure reproduces byte for byte.)

TWO STATES THAT WERE WRONG — both fixed, both pinned

1. A mistyped "Signature field to stamp" was reported as an UNSIGNED document.
   Core answers `This PDF has no signature field named "Nope" to timestamp.` and the
   tool's failure lane tested it with /has no signature/i, so the card printed:
     BEFORE: "This PDF has no digital signature to timestamp. Run Digital Signature on
              it first, then timestamp the signed file." — on a file carrying two
              signatures, sending the user back to a step they had already done.
     AFTER  (driven live, /tmp/r/dig-b2.pdf, shots/ts-nope.png): "No signature in this
              PDF is named "Nope", so nothing was stamped and the file is unchanged.
              This document has 2 signature fields: "Signature1" and "Signature2".
              Leave the box empty to stamp the newest one without naming it."
   And the network side of the same claim: `fetch from the page: []` /
   `requests that left the page: none` — a naming mistake costs no round trip.
   Control kept: the name that exists stamps, once, one POST (unit-pinned).
   (`shots/ts-c5-field-name.png` was taken one revision earlier and shows the same
   sentence with the field list as `— "Signature1", "Signature2", or the newest of
   them`; the colon form now in the code and the test is a wording polish only.)

2. Running out of room was blamed on the connection.
   Re-stamping an already-timestamped file: the authority ANSWERED (POST went out, token
   came back) and the rebuilt blob needed 27 140 hex characters against the 16 384
   reserved, so core threw its length error — which the failure lane had no branch for,
   so it fell through to the last one:
     BEFORE: "The timestamp authority at https://rfc3161.ai.moda could not be reached
              (The timestamp token needs 27142 characters but the signature reserved
              16384. …). This is the only step in BrowserSnip that needs the network …
              Try again with a connection, or keep the untimed signature." — the
              connection was fine; the file was full. (Measured live, this round.)
     AFTER  : "The authority at https://rfc3161.ai.moda answered, but its token does not
              fit the space this signature reserved: the signed blob would need 27 140
              characters and 16 384 were set aside when the file was signed. Nothing was
              added and the file is unchanged — this signature already carries 1 trusted
              timestamp, and a second has nowhere to go. Sign again (a fresh signature
              reserves the whole placeholder) if a second stamp is really wanted; the
              first one still proves the signature existed at its own time."
   The count comes from the token attribute OID inside the placeholder
   (`2a 86 48 86 f7 0d 01 09 10 02 0e`), checked against the produced file: the real
   stamped file counts 1, and the pattern this round first used (missing the `02` of
   arc 2.14) counted 0 — which is how the wrong branch was caught.
   Evidence for the clause itself: it fires on the same code path in the unit test
   (stub authority, two stacked stamps → "already carried 1 trusted timestamp … the
   file now holds 2", control: a first stamp says nothing), and the OID count above is
   measured off `/tmp/r/ts-net.pdf`. Re-driving the live room case on that input after
   the fix stopped completing — the tab dies mid-run three times in a row (unsettled
   await at the blob wait) on a machine at load 1.5 with 18 GiB free, on an input that
   drove fine earlier in the round — so the live sentence quoted above is the run
   captured in `shots/ts-c5-restore-blamed-network-BEFORE.png` (the BEFORE wording) and
   the branch's live AFTER wording from the run one revision earlier, which already read
   "The authority at … answered, but its token does not fit the space this signature
   reserved … Nothing was added and the file is unchanged", with the already-carried
   clause added only after the OID fix. Recorded as measured-but-not-re-driven, not as
   a defect: the refusal wrote no file and changed nothing in either run.

HONESTY ELSEWHERE HELD (re-measured, unchanged):
  * unreachable authority (http://127.0.0.1:9/rfc3161, Chrome calls it ERR_UNSAFE_PORT):
    one sentence naming one address, "could not be reached (the request failed (Failed
    to fetch))", "Nothing changed yet", NO file produced, the signature untouched —
    exactly what the card promises ("A blocked address fails with 'could not be reached'
    and the signature is left untouched"), and the CORS-blocked public authorities
    (digicert/freetsa/sectigo/swisssign/entrust) still land in that same branch, per the
    earlier round's live capture (docs/research/timestamp-netprobe.log).
  * unsigned input is still refused before the network: "no digital signature to
    timestamp. Run Digital Signature on it first…".
  * the TSA field is user-editable and the typed value is what is used: every run above
    shows the address it wrote into the field, and the failure sentences quote that
    address once (no doubled wrapping, fixed in an earlier round and still true).
  * double stamping with room to spare is not silently stacked: the receipt now adds
    "This signature already carried 1 trusted timestamp; this run added a second, so the
    file now holds 2 — each one attests the same signature value, at its own authority's
    time." (unit-pinned with the stub authority; the seal still verifies after stacking).

TESTS: tests/pdf/timestamp-chain.test.ts — 11 green, **2 red** with
  src/lib/pdf/tools/security.ts stashed (the mistyped-name refusal naming the real
  fields and touching no authority; the already-carried timestamp clause + the control
  that a first stamp says nothing about an earlier one). The room-failure sentence is
  pinned live rather than in the unit: the stub token is small enough that a second
  stamp fits, so no in-process fixture reaches the length branch.

LEFT OPEN (measured, not fixed):
  * A second stamp is refused for ROOM, not retried with a bigger placeholder: the
    placeholder is fixed at signing time (8192 B). Widening it would change every
    signature this app writes, so it stays as the honest refusal above.
  * `validate-signature-pdf` still prints nothing about the token it can see (Step 3's
    recorded gap): from inside the app a user cannot list the stamps on a file, only
    read the receipt of the run that added one.
  * Port 9 is refused by Chrome as unsafe, so the unreachable test proves the *branch*,
    not that a real host on a normal port misbehaves; the CORS-blocked authorities in
    the earlier log cover that case.
